1
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:14,911
By the winter of twenty thirty-two, Umbrella Life
Sciences was worth six hundred billion

2
00:00:14,931 --> 00:00:15,451
dollars.

3
00:00:16,021 --> 00:00:21,910
Aeterna had become the best-selling medicine in the
history of the world. The assay was

4
00:00:21,930 --> 00:00:28,090
in eighty thousand hospitals. The rapid-response
platform had delivered, for the Department

5
00:00:28,110 --> 00:00:33,932
of Defense, a vaccine against a synthetic test
pathogen in eight days — one fewer than

6
00:00:33,952 --> 00:00:39,977
promised — and the Pentagon had quietly doubled the
contract. Umbrella employed forty-one

7
00:00:39,997 --> 00:00:45,886
thousand people on four continents, and every one of
them had put a hand on their chest

8
00:00:45,906 --> 00:00:49,418
on their first morning and said: we
are the shelter.

9
00:00:49,987 --> 00:00:52,177
And the Director had begun to eat.

10
00:00:54,247 --> 00:00:58,429
The first company he ate was the one that
had tried to steal from him.

11
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:05,241
Halvorsen Pike was the second-largest pharmaceutical
company on Earth — a Swiss-American

12
00:01:05,261 --> 00:01:11,929
giant, headquartered in New Jersey and in Basel, with a
hundred and eighty thousand employees,

13
00:01:11,949 --> 00:01:18,191
three hundred billion dollars of market value, and a
hundred and forty years of history.

14
00:01:18,211 --> 00:01:24,381
It had made the first mass-produced penicillin in
Europe. It had made the vaccines that

15
00:01:24,401 --> 00:01:30,643
ended polio in forty countries. It had made, in the
nineteen-nineties, a heart drug that

16
00:01:30,663 --> 00:01:34,628
had kept one in every twenty
Americans over sixty alive.

17
00:01:35,197 --> 00:01:40,891
And in the autumn of twenty twenty-nine, through
three layers of consultants and a man

18
00:01:40,911 --> 00:01:47,004
named Simon Ferris, it had paid four men with rifles to
break into a building in Wilmington,

19
00:01:47,024 --> 00:01:50,857
where Howard Lessing had been shot
dead against a freezer.

20
00:01:53,048 --> 00:01:55,438
They killed one of ours.

21
00:01:55,957 --> 00:01:59,433
They killed a man who was betraying
us. For money. To them.

22
00:01:59,954 --> 00:02:03,915
He was ours. He died in our
building. That is a debt.

23
00:02:04,436 --> 00:02:06,016
For once, Adolf, we agree.

24
00:02:08,036 --> 00:02:14,065
Halvorsen Pike was run by a woman named Ingrid
Halvorsen, and the company carried her

25
00:02:14,085 --> 00:02:15,844
great-grandfather's name.

26
00:02:16,414 --> 00:02:21,882
She was sixty-four. She had been born in Bergen,
trained as a heart surgeon in Oslo and

27
00:02:21,902 --> 00:02:27,244
at Johns Hopkins, and had operated for sixteen
years before she moved into the family

28
00:02:27,264 --> 00:02:32,732
business, because her brother had died and there was
no one else. She had run Halvorsen

29
00:02:32,752 --> 00:02:38,093
Pike for sixteen years. She was brilliant, and
cold, and completely fearless, and she

30
00:02:38,113 --> 00:02:43,644
had faced down governments, activist investors, and
the European Commission, and she had

31
00:02:43,664 --> 00:02:44,653
never once lost.

32
00:02:45,223 --> 00:02:51,059
She had not known about the break-in. That was the
truth, and the Director knew it. Simon

33
00:02:51,079 --> 00:02:56,783
Ferris had gone through a budget she never saw, to a
consultant she had never heard of,

34
00:02:56,803 --> 00:03:02,375
and she had learned about Nevada and Wilmington
only when a young man from Umbrella's

35
00:03:02,395 --> 00:03:08,098
digital department had arranged — anonymously — for
a national newspaper to learn about

36
00:03:08,118 --> 00:03:08,690
it first.

37
00:03:09,260 --> 00:03:15,103
It did not matter. She was the chief executive. It
was her company. The debt was hers.

38
00:03:17,174 --> 00:03:21,891
They met in Davos, in January, because
everyone meets in Davos.

39
00:03:22,461 --> 00:03:27,838
It was the week of the World Economic Forum, when
the chief executives of the thousand

40
00:03:27,858 --> 00:03:33,359
largest companies on Earth, and a few dozen heads of
government, and a great many people

41
00:03:33,379 --> 00:03:38,818
who wanted to stand near them, gather in a small ski
town in the Swiss Alps to discuss,

42
00:03:38,838 --> 00:03:44,465
in panels and at dinners and in private chalets, how
the world should be run. The Director

43
00:03:44,485 --> 00:03:50,175
had been invited for the first time. He had declined
to speak on any panel. He had accepted

44
00:03:50,195 --> 00:03:51,556
every private meeting.

45
00:03:52,126 --> 00:03:58,365
Ingrid Halvorsen received him in a private chalet
above the town, at thirteen at night,

46
00:03:58,385 --> 00:04:02,899
by a fire. She did not offer him a
drink. She did not stand up.

47
00:04:03,588 --> 00:04:08,137
Let me save us both some time, Doctor Wesker. I
know why you're here. You're going to

48
00:04:08,157 --> 00:04:12,868
tell me that Umbrella would like to acquire my
company. You're going to call it a merger

49
00:04:12,888 --> 00:04:17,221
of equals, and we both know there is no such
thing. And I'm going to tell you no.

50
00:04:17,742 --> 00:04:18,973
That's very efficient.

51
00:04:19,492 --> 00:04:22,758
I was a surgeon. We don't talk while
the patient bleeds.

52
00:04:24,278 --> 00:04:26,882
I wasn't going to call it a
merger of equals.

53
00:04:27,401 --> 00:04:28,170
No?

54
00:04:28,690 --> 00:04:33,171
I was going to call it what it is. You owe me a
debt. I've come to collect it.

55
00:04:35,192 --> 00:04:41,041
She looked at him for a long moment. He had taken off
his sunglasses, because the chalet

56
00:04:41,061 --> 00:04:46,777
was dim, and the Cold-Read was already giving him
Ingrid Halvorsen in pieces — and the

57
00:04:46,797 --> 00:04:52,446
pieces were extraordinary. There was no fear. There
was anger, very controlled. There

58
00:04:52,466 --> 00:04:58,448
was guilt, which surprised him — genuine guilt, for a
dead man in Wilmington she had never

59
00:04:58,468 --> 00:05:04,517
met. And there was something beneath it that he had
seen perhaps three times in this world.

60
00:05:04,537 --> 00:05:10,386
Pride. Not vanity. Pride, of the old kind, that a
person takes in something they did not

61
00:05:10,406 --> 00:05:12,920
build but have been entrusted to keep.

62
00:05:13,610 --> 00:05:18,552
If you mean Wilmington — I did not know. The man
responsible no longer works for me. He

63
00:05:18,572 --> 00:05:23,514
is cooperating with the prosecutors in New Jersey. I
have made my own apology to Doctor

64
00:05:23,534 --> 00:05:28,589
Lessing's widow, privately, and she has accepted it.
And the company has paid her a great

65
00:05:28,609 --> 00:05:31,041
deal of money, which she has
also accepted.

66
00:05:31,562 --> 00:05:32,246
I know.

67
00:05:32,766 --> 00:05:34,718
Then you know there is no debt.

68
00:05:35,237 --> 00:05:41,168
There's always a debt, Doctor Halvorsen. The only
question is who decides when it's been

69
00:05:41,188 --> 00:05:41,507
paid.

70
00:05:43,526 --> 00:05:49,701
She did not look away. Very few people ever managed
that, at a range of eight feet, with

71
00:05:49,721 --> 00:05:55,684
his glasses off. She managed it for a full ten
seconds, and when she spoke again, her

72
00:05:55,704 --> 00:05:57,725
voice had not changed at all.

73
00:05:58,415 --> 00:06:03,664
My great-grandfather started this company in a barn
outside Bergen in eighteen ninety-two.

74
00:06:03,684 --> 00:06:08,639
It has survived two world wars, the Spanish flu,
the Depression, four recessions, and

75
00:06:08,659 --> 00:06:13,673
a German occupation of Norway during which he hid a
laboratory in a fish-canning plant

76
00:06:13,693 --> 00:06:19,058
rather than let them have it. It is not going to be
eaten by a man who appeared from nowhere

77
00:06:19,078 --> 00:06:22,161
five years ago and has, I am told,
very unusual eyes.

78
00:06:24,180 --> 00:06:29,681
The Director smiled. It was a genuine smile, and
the Cold-Read would have told anyone

79
00:06:29,701 --> 00:06:33,189
who could see it that it held a
great deal of respect.

80
00:06:33,879 --> 00:06:38,674
I'm going to take your company, Ingrid. I'm going to
take it in the open, with a letter

81
00:06:38,694 --> 00:06:43,545
and a vote, according to every law in every country
it operates in. You'll fight. You'll

82
00:06:43,565 --> 00:06:48,250
fight very well. And you'll lose. I wanted you to
hear that from me, here, by a fire,

83
00:06:48,270 --> 00:06:51,350
and not from your general counsel at
six in the morning.

84
00:06:51,870 --> 00:06:53,462
How very courteous.

85
00:06:53,982 --> 00:06:59,532
I find that the people I respect should be told the
truth. It's the only luxury I can

86
00:06:59,552 --> 00:07:00,319
afford them.

87
00:07:02,838 --> 00:07:05,500
The letter went out on the
third of February.

88
00:07:06,069 --> 00:07:11,528
It was addressed to the board of directors of
Halvorsen Pike, and it was released to the

89
00:07:11,548 --> 00:07:17,132
press at the same moment it was delivered — which is
what bankers call a bear hug, because

90
00:07:17,152 --> 00:07:22,424
it is an embrace that you cannot get out of. It
offered Halvorsen Pike's shareholders

91
00:07:22,444 --> 00:07:27,965
one share of Umbrella for every two and a half of
theirs: a premium of forty-one percent,

92
00:07:27,985 --> 00:07:33,257
worth four hundred and twenty billion dollars, the
largest corporate acquisition ever

93
00:07:33,277 --> 00:07:33,818
proposed.

94
00:07:34,387 --> 00:07:40,686
Halvorsen Pike's shares rose thirty-two percent before
lunch. The board rejected the offer

95
00:07:40,706 --> 00:07:46,864
by five in the afternoon. By Friday, it had adopted a
shareholder rights plan — a poison

96
00:07:46,884 --> 00:07:52,902
pill, which would flood the market with new shares
if any single buyer crossed fifteen

97
00:07:52,922 --> 00:07:57,044
percent, and make any hostile takeover
ruinously expensive.

98
00:07:57,615 --> 00:08:03,320
Catherine Adeyemi read the rights plan at her desk
on the eleventh floor, and laughed,

99
00:08:03,340 --> 00:08:06,982
once, and said to her team: good.
Now it's interesting.

100
00:08:09,052 --> 00:08:15,606
The war that followed lasted six weeks, and it was
fought, as all such wars are now fought,

101
00:08:15,626 --> 00:08:17,702
almost entirely in documents.

102
00:08:18,271 --> 00:08:24,088
Catherine ran it from a glass war room on the
eleventh floor, with thirty analysts, four

103
00:08:24,108 --> 00:08:29,991
investment banks, three law firms, and the particular
brilliance of a woman who had spent

104
00:08:30,011 --> 00:08:35,895
her whole career watching men like Ingrid Halvorsen
defend companies like Halvorsen Pike,

105
00:08:35,915 --> 00:08:41,864
and knew every move before it was made. If the poison
pill stopped Umbrella buying shares,

106
00:08:41,884 --> 00:08:47,634
then Umbrella would not buy shares. It would buy
votes. Halvorsen Pike's annual meeting

107
00:08:47,654 --> 00:08:53,471
was in May. At it, four of the company's twelve
directors were up for election. Umbrella

108
00:08:53,491 --> 00:08:59,175
would nominate four of its own, and persuade the
shareholders — the pension funds, the

109
00:08:59,195 --> 00:09:04,946
index funds, the sovereign wealth funds that owned
most of the company — to elect them.

110
00:09:04,966 --> 00:09:10,915
And once Umbrella's directors were in the room, they
would make the case, from the inside,

111
00:09:10,935 --> 00:09:12,375
for removing the pill.

112
00:09:12,945 --> 00:09:19,001
It was called a proxy fight. It was the oldest weapon
in corporate America. And Catherine

113
00:09:19,021 --> 00:09:22,210
had never enjoyed anything so
much in her life.

114
00:09:23,779 --> 00:09:29,541
That was the thing she noticed, one night in March,
alone in the war room at two in the

115
00:09:29,561 --> 00:09:34,458
morning, looking at her reflection in the dark
glass. She was enjoying it.

116
00:09:35,028 --> 00:09:40,202
She had joined Umbrella five years before to be close
to the most interesting bet of her

117
00:09:40,222 --> 00:09:45,336
life. She had stayed after the money in the ground
because she wanted to be on the side

118
00:09:45,356 --> 00:09:50,706
that won. And now, standing in a glass room at two in
the morning, planning the destruction

119
00:09:50,726 --> 00:09:55,723
of a hundred-and-forty-year-old company whose chief
executive had done nothing to her

120
00:09:55,743 --> 00:10:00,739
but refuse to be eaten, she found that she was not
thinking about Ingrid Halvorsen at

121
00:10:00,759 --> 00:10:02,923
all. She was thinking about the vote.

122
00:10:03,493 --> 00:10:08,862
She thought about a white card, in her wallet,
behind her own. She did not take it out.

123
00:10:10,932 --> 00:10:16,581
The Director's contribution to the war was smaller,
and uglier, and it came in April.

124
00:10:17,150 --> 00:10:23,002
Halvorsen Pike's shareholders had been leaning toward
Ingrid Halvorsen. She was admired.

125
00:10:23,022 --> 00:10:28,673
The company was old and trusted. And the index
funds, which owned a third of it, were

126
00:10:28,693 --> 00:10:34,544
reluctant to hand the second-largest pharmaceutical
company on Earth to a man whose most

127
00:10:34,564 --> 00:10:40,348
famous public appearance was eleven seconds of
silence and whose second most famous was

128
00:10:40,368 --> 00:10:41,749
a smear on a dashcam.

129
00:10:42,320 --> 00:10:48,758
So Tomás arranged for the prosecutors' files in New
Jersey — the ones about Simon Ferris,

130
00:10:48,778 --> 00:10:55,361
and the consultants, and the four men with rifles — to
be read by a reporter at the largest

131
00:10:55,381 --> 00:10:59,860
financial newspaper in the world, three
weeks before the vote.

132
00:11:00,429 --> 00:11:06,707
The story ran on a Monday. Its headline did not
accuse Ingrid Halvorsen of anything. It

133
00:11:06,727 --> 00:11:13,150
did not need to. It simply put her company's name in
the same sentence as the words armed

134
00:11:13,170 --> 00:11:18,072
intruders and dead scientist, and let the
pension funds do the rest.

135
00:11:18,643 --> 00:11:25,098
Halvorsen Pike's stock fell nine percent. Two of the
three largest index funds announced

136
00:11:25,118 --> 00:11:28,778
that they would, quote, support
board refreshment.

137
00:11:30,966 --> 00:11:32,398
That was cruel.

138
00:11:32,918 --> 00:11:35,988
That was accurate. Everything in
that article is true.

139
00:11:36,508 --> 00:11:42,056
That is what made it cruel. You have learned my
best trick, and dressed it in a suit.

140
00:11:42,576 --> 00:11:47,434
Your best trick was lies. Mine is the truth,
arranged. It lasts longer.

141
00:11:49,955 --> 00:11:55,561
Ingrid Halvorsen fought back the only way she had
left. She went looking for somebody

142
00:11:55,581 --> 00:11:56,951
who knew what he was.

143
00:11:57,519 --> 00:12:03,674
It took her private investigators nine days to find
the man. He was living alone by then,

144
00:12:03,694 --> 00:12:09,849
in a small apartment in Chester, working nights. He
answered his phone on the first ring,

145
00:12:09,869 --> 00:12:11,375
because he always did.

146
00:12:12,066 --> 00:12:17,342
Mister Carrick. My name is Ingrid Halvorsen. I run a
pharmaceutical company that Albert

147
00:12:17,362 --> 00:12:19,290
Wesker is about to take from me.

148
00:12:19,809 --> 00:12:21,065
I know who you are.

149
00:12:21,586 --> 00:12:26,088
I've read everything that was ever written about
you. The dismissal. The bank account.

150
00:12:26,108 --> 00:12:30,610
The bullets. I've read it all, and I've read it the
way a surgeon reads a chart. And I

151
00:12:30,630 --> 00:12:34,239
have concluded, Mister Carrick, that you
were not having a breakdown.

152
00:12:36,259 --> 00:12:40,091
Owen Carrick sat down very slowly on
the edge of his bed.

153
00:12:40,660 --> 00:12:46,642
In fourteen months, he had shown the bullets to four
journalists, two members of Congress,

154
00:12:46,662 --> 00:12:52,444
a Pentagon official, and a priest. Every one of them
had been kind. Not one of them had

155
00:12:52,464 --> 00:12:54,312
said what she had just said.

156
00:12:55,003 --> 00:12:56,736
(soft) Why are you calling me?

157
00:12:57,255 --> 00:13:02,057
Because I am going to lose this vote. And when I lose
it, he will own the second-largest

158
00:13:02,077 --> 00:13:06,988
pharmaceutical company on Earth, and a hundred and
eighty thousand people, and forty years

159
00:13:07,008 --> 00:13:11,809
of research that my family has spent a century
building. And I have decided that I would

160
00:13:11,829 --> 00:13:15,041
rather spend what I have left of it
finding out what he is.

161
00:13:16,942 --> 00:13:21,611
You said once — I read it in a deposition — that
someone would have to stop him who wasn't

162
00:13:21,631 --> 00:13:26,145
you. I'm not you, Mister Carrick. I have twelve
billion dollars in cash, and lawyers in

163
00:13:26,165 --> 00:13:30,626
forty countries, and friends in the European
Commission. And I would like to hire you.

164
00:13:33,147 --> 00:13:38,409
Carrick looked at the jam jar on the windowsill,
where fifteen pieces of flattened lead

165
00:13:38,429 --> 00:13:40,838
caught the orange light from the street.

166
00:13:41,408 --> 00:13:47,035
He had told Hannah Price, six months before, that
they would need to find someone who

167
00:13:47,055 --> 00:13:53,214
was not them. He had not imagined it would be a
Norwegian heart surgeon with a pharmaceutical

168
00:13:53,234 --> 00:13:53,745
company.

169
00:13:54,315 --> 00:13:59,859
He had not imagined, either, that the voice at the
back of his mind — the careerist, the

170
00:13:59,879 --> 00:14:05,234
one that had wanted to go to Washington, the one he
had thought was dead — would stir

171
00:14:05,254 --> 00:14:09,534
at the sound of nine billion dollars, and
sit up, and pay attention.

172
00:14:10,225 --> 00:14:11,307
When do I start?
